; Tutorial 2.01: Graphics and the Main Loop
;
; Every game opens a graphics mode and then runs a main loop: once per frame it reads the input, updates the game
; and draws a new picture. This tutorial shows that loop with a square that slides across the window.
;
; Works on: the original Blitz3D and BlitzPlus, and BlitzLLVM.

; Graphics width, height, depth, mode
;   depth 0 picks the best colour depth by itself.
;   mode 2 is a window. Mode 1 would be fullscreen, and mode 0 lets Blitz choose (a window while debugging).
Graphics 640, 480, 0, 2
AppTitle "Tutorial 2.01: Graphics and the Main Loop"

; Blitz draws into one of two pictures: the front buffer is what you see, the back buffer is hidden. Drawing into
; the back buffer and showing it all at once with Flip means the player never sees a half-drawn frame.
SetBuffer BackBuffer()

Local x# = 0
Local frames = 0

; The main loop. KeyHit(1) is true once each time Escape (scan code 1) is pressed, so Escape ends the loop.
While Not KeyHit(1)
	; 1. Update: move the square, and wrap it around when it leaves the window.
	x = x + 2.5
	If x > GraphicsWidth() Then x = -40
	frames = frames + 1

	; 2. Draw: clear the back buffer, then draw the new frame into it.
	ClsColor 20, 24, 48 ; The colour Cls fills with.
	Cls
	Color 255, 200, 60
	Rect Int(x), 220, 40, 40, 1
	Color 255, 255, 255
	Text 10, 10, "Frame " + frames + " - press Escape to quit"
	Text 10, 30, "The window is " + GraphicsWidth() + " x " + GraphicsHeight() + " pixels."

	; 3. Show: swap the buffers. Flip waits for the monitor's next refresh (vertical sync), which also keeps the
	;    loop from running faster than the screen can show. Tutorial 2.04 makes the speed independent of the screen.
	Flip
Wend

; EndGraphics closes the graphics mode. End would do that by itself as well.
EndGraphics
End
